{"custom_id": "53b65d3a19b61f2319ed5f903d36284ef3f4943420d60ca9", "meta": {"loop": "topk4L01", "task": "infer_discrepancy_patterns_2", "occurrence": 0, "attempt": 0, "max_tokens": 128000, "prompt_sha256": "4b6917103741b5d6958605f3621ff98a0bffa4922bb6fdfe164df224dc5c9056", "prompt_chars": 11099}, "batch_id": "msgbatch_01T2WamyzkWEiUKcRdRasvsK", "saved_utc": "2026-10-22T11:17:05+00:00", "message": {"id": "msg_011CgHBEpWgAf1CZKjtkYBus", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "I'm going through the mismatches where gold labeled something DiseaseClass but the LLM said O — things like abnormal retinal vascular development described as a disease feature, norrin deficiency as a molecular state, and ichthyosis appearing in a symptom list.\n\nI notice \"mental retardation\" in that same list was correctly matched as DiseaseClass, while \"ichthyosis\" and \"spasticity\" in the identical syntactic position weren't — so the model is being inconsistent within one enumeration. Meanwhile \"exudative retinal detachment\" was correctly caught as a true positive, since it's a concrete named clinical condition rather than a process description, which suggests the model struggles more with phenotypic descriptions and symptom lists than with clearly named disease entities.\n\nAll four discrepancies share a common frame: they're the object or complement of a disease-characterizing predicate like \"characterized by,\" \"results in,\" or \"secondary to\" — and they're symptoms, pathological processes, or molecular deficiencies rather than canonical disease names, so the model treats them as descriptive background rather than tagging them as DiseaseClass. The contrast with \"mental retardation\" (correctly tagged in the same list) and \"exudative retinal detachment\" (a parenthetical named pathology) shows the model leans on recognizable disease-name lexicon rather than the predicate-argument structure itself to decide what counts as an entity.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "**Step 1: Deep Dive Analysis**\n\n- **Contrast:** The four discrepancies are all disease-manifestation phrases (a symptom, a pathological process, or a molecular deficiency state) in sentences that describe what the disease *is* or *does*: \"Coats disease is characterized by…\", \"…which results in a deficiency of norrin\", \"…is an autosomal recessive disorder characterized by ichthyosis, mental retardation, spasticity…\". The True Positive DiseaseClass cases include \"mental retardation\" from the same \"characterized by\" list and \"exudative retinal detachment\" as a named clinical condition. The True Positive Modifiers, such as \"ovarian cancer\" in \"family history of ovarian cancer,\" are attributive modifiers of another noun.\n- **Isolate Trigger:** The model appears to treat clinical-description complements as background prose. Within one list, it captured \"mental retardation\" but missed \"ichthyosis\" and \"spasticity,\" which suggests it is not applying the characterization frame consistently. Single-word symptom nouns and multi-word process nouns (\"abnormal retinal vascular development,\" \"deficiency of norrin\") lack the disease-name lexicon it relies on.\n- **Hypothesis Check:** The trigger explains all four discrepancies: each sits in a characterization or causal frame, and each is a manifestation rather than a disease name. Refinement is needed for two points. First, \"deficient activity of fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase\" in the same sentence as Example 4 is unlabeled, so the rule must exclude enzyme or biochemical measurements that are not framed as manifestations. Second, the gold labeling is not fully consistent across documents, so the rule should be stated at the level of the manifestation frame.\n\n**Step 2: Formulate Pattern**\n\n- **Pattern Insight:**\n  - **Pattern Name:** Disease manifestations in characterization or causal frames labeled as DiseaseClass.\n  - **Confusion Trigger:** The span is a symptom, sign, pathological process, or disease-state deficiency nominal that is the complement of a disease-describing predicate (\"characterized by,\" \"results in,\" \"secondary to\") or a parenthetical gloss of the disease's pathology. Because it is not a formal disease name, the model treats it as descriptive context and assigns O.\n  - **Contrastive Evidence:** The True Positive \"mental retardation\" appears in the same \"characterized by\" list as the missed \"ichthyosis\" and \"spasticity,\" so the frame alone can be recognized. \"Exudative retinal detachment\" is captured as a named clinical condition with a canonical disorder head noun. The Modifier True Positives are attributive noun modifiers of a disease mention, a different syntactic role.\n  - **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** Label a noun phrase as DiseaseClass when (a) its head denotes a clinical manifestation (symptom, sign, pathological process, or disease-state deficiency) of a SpecificDisease mention, and (b) it appears in a characterization or causal frame (\"X is characterized by [list],\" \"[cause] results in [N],\" \"secondary to … [N]\") or as a parenthetical gloss of the disease's pathology. Do not label it when it is an attributive modifier of a disease mention (e.g., \"G6PD deficient phenotype\" → Modifier) or a biochemical measurement with no manifestation framing (e.g., \"deficient activity of FALDH\" → O). Adjectival modifiers such as \"abnormal\" do not disqualify a span.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4351, "output_tokens": 7074, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 5899}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}